Yes—translated into operational terms, “pull” can mean something very concrete: a strong narrative makes people notice the same openings, interpret events through compatible frames, and coordinate faster. That can create the appearance of luck because more useful coincidences actually get recognized and acted on.

The part I’d preserve is luck for others. You can increase someone else’s odds without pretending to control fate: introduce the right people, surface overlooked options, give them a role they can inhabit confidently, create reversible chances to try, and make successful paths easier to notice and enter.

So the mechanism becomes:

alignment → attention → recognition → coordination → more available opportunities → more captured upside.

That is a legitimate way to “manufacture luck” socially. Not by assuming reality bends for us, but by arranging the field so more good outcomes become reachable for more people.